.Rproj.user
.Rhistory
.RData
.Ruserdata
.DS_Store
Rplots.pdf
setwd.R
*/
!/data/
!/tests/
!/tests/**
!/mlos_review/
# The */ rule above ignores every directory, so CI has to be let back in by
# name the way data/, tests/ and mlos_review/ are.
!/.github/
!/.github/**
/tests/results/
__pycache__/

# ShelterDataPrep writes three files per dataset: <name>_data.csv, its
# <name>_data_stats.csv processing ledger, and a <name>_data_run.txt log.
# The prepared data and the ledger are tracked -- both are small, and the
# ledger is the exclusion accounting behind the numbers.
# The log is not: it records absolute paths, local timestamps and library
# versions, so it differs on every run and every machine.
/data/*_run.txt

# Raw shelter extracts are not kept here at all. They are large, they are
# published data, and they belong in a deposit with their own DOI rather than
# in git history, where every version would live forever. The extract behind
# OC1 and OC2 is deposited at 10.5281/zenodo.22051091.
/data/*_raw.csv.gz

# The Word exports of the guides are tracked, and make_docx.sh builds them.
# Neither was true until 2026-08-23. A .docx is a zip archive, so it
# neither diffs nor delta-compresses, and 74 commits of them had grown to a
# third of the repository's packed size, which is why they were untracked in
# the first place. What makes tracking them safe is the rule that replaced the
# old habit: they are rebuilt for a release, not on every edit, so the history
# gains a few hundred kilobytes a release rather than a few hundred a day.
# Do not commit a rebuild alongside an ordinary documentation change.
#
# Whether a .docx matches its .md is answered by the version stamp both carry
# and by the source commit make_docx.sh writes into the .docx, not by
# modification time: python3 tests/show_doc_versions.py.

# templates/ is covered by the */ rule above, and that is load-bearing rather
# than incidental. A deck template is somebody else's file: an organization's
# branding, carrying their logos and their sponsors' marks, lent for one talk.
# Committing one would publish it. Keep templates there and point at them with
# `template:` in a deck settings file or with --template on the command line;
# the outlines that describe a variant deck are yours and live in data/.

# tools/ is already covered by the */ rule above, and stays that way. It holds
# make_deposit.py, which stages the Zenodo deposits described in
# PUBLISHING.md: a local packaging step run between a release and an upload,
# not part of what an analysis run needs.
tests/**/.DS_Store
tests/**/.Rhistory
